d_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Persp
ectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252", "content": "1.   The 
Ring of Nestor, measuring 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, is the fifth largest Aegean 
signet ring.\n2.   Research confirms its authenticity, contrasting earlier 
claims of forgery by scholars in the 1920s.\n3.   The scorpion, identified as a
significant apotropaic symbol, plays a crucial role in the ring's 
iconography.\n4.   Recent findings enhance the understanding of Aegean glyptic 
art from the Late Bronze Age (16th-15th century BC). [...] This paper focuses 
on a carnelian lentoid (M6621), housed in the Cabinet des Médailles of the 
Bibliothèque nationale de France in Paris, which was rejected as a forgery by 
Henri and Micheline van Effenterre when they compiled CMS IX (1972). The study 
presents a summary account of the collection's history, together with an 
assessment of the scholarly milieu in which the authors of the volume worked. 
There follows a detailed evaluation of the seal in question, in which material,
shape, size, [...] View more arrow_downward\n\n## FAQs\n\nImage 12: 
sparkles\nAI\n\nWhat does the Ring of Nestor reveal about ancient Aegean gold 
craftsmanship?add\n\nThe study shows that the Ring of Nestor possesses 97% gold
content, indicating high-quality Ancient Aegean craftsmanship comparable to 
notable pieces like the Archanes Ring and the Ring of Mycenae.\n\nHow does the 
Ring of Nestor's iconography compare to other Aegean artifacts?add", 
