"Excavations - Griffin Warrior", "url": 
"https://griffinwarrior.org/excavations", "content": "Griffin Warrior\n\nSelect
Page\n\nGriffin Warrior Tomb\n\nTholos Tombs\n\nTsakonas 
Field\n\nRomanou\n\nAcropolis\n\nAlthough the current project has been 
operating at the site since 2015, the Palace of Nestor has been the focus of 
archaeological excavations for much longer. As each excavation season passes, 
the story of the area continues to unfold.\n\n#### 1912\n\nNorth of the bay of 
Navarino, the Greek Archaeological Service’s Dr. Konstantinos Kourouniotis and 
his team excavate a tholos tomb.\n\n#### 1926 [...] #### 1940\n\nWWII prevents 
an intended second excavation season at the palace.\n\n#### 
1945\n\nKourouniotis passes away, and Professor Spyridon Marinatos is named as 
his replacement. Marinatos will go on to prefer working on sites surrounding 
the palace, leaving the palace excavation and cleaning to the Cincinnati 
team.\n\n#### 1952 - 1966\n\nAcross fifteen excavation seasons, the Cincinnati 
team uncovers the Palace of Nestor and the adjacent acropolis.\n\n#### 1960 - 
1961 [...] #### 1939\n\nWith the help of local residents, namely Charalambos 
Christophilopoulos of Koryfasio, the joint expedition finds seven or eight 
Mycenaean sites in ten days.\n\n#### April 4, 1939", "raw_content_chars": 5288,
"published_at": null, "score": 0.4295017}, {"title": "Pylos of Nestor A 
Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed - National Archaeological Museum", "url": 
"https://www.namuseum.gr/en/temporary_exhibition/pylos-of-nestor-a-mycenaean-ki
ngdom-revealed", "content": "# Εθνικό Αρχαιολογικό Μουσείο Εθνικό Αρχαιολογικό 
Μουσείο\n\nΕθνικό Αρχαιολογικό Μουσείο\nΕθνικό Αρχαιολογικό Μουσείο\n\nMAY 
2026\n\nTUESDAY\n\nOCT 2026\n\nSATURDAY\n\n## Pylos of Nestor A Mycenaean 
Kingdom Revealed\n\n#### Hellenic National Archaeological Museum\n\nMay 26th – 
October 31st, 2026\n\n#### A Journey in Progress. Kalamata → Los Angeles → 
Athens [...] In 1939, Carl W. Blegen and Konstantinos Kouroυniotis uncovered 
one of the most important Mycenaean palaces in mainland Greece during 
excavations in Messenia. This Palace was identified as the Homeric Pylos of 
King Nestor. Built shortly after 1300 BCE, it served as the administrative 
center of a powerful kingdom that controlled almost the entire region of 
modern-day Messenia. [...] The exhibition “The Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean 
Kingdom Revealed” is presented at the Hellenic National Archaeological Museum 
as the third station in a journey that began at the Archaeological Museum of 
Messenia (February–May 2025) and moved on at the J. Paul Getty Museum.", 
