commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 
'b1-519d8d22', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is the name commonly given to a 
gold signet ring that entered scholarly literature in 1925 and became 
associated with interpretations of Minoan imagery and the afterlife.[1][2] Sir 
Arthur Evans published a 1925 study titled *The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into 
the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and 
Bead-Seals from Thisbe, Boeotia*.[3] The ring’s reported discovery in 1924 and 
its association with a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos are not supported in
the supplied material by a professionally documented excavation record.[4][5] 
Its authenticity has remained disputed: Evans accepted it and used its imagery 
in a Minoan afterlife interpretation, while members of the archaeological board
in Athens reportedly rejected it as a modern forgery.[5][6] A later 
peer-reviewed article by Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson argued that the 
ring’s butterfly motif weighs against authenticity, although the available 
abstract does not provide the full iconographic analysis.[7] These unresolved 
questions place the object within the category *gemma dubitanda*, a technical 
term for sealstones and signet rings attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age whose 
provenance is not securely documented.[8]', 'evidence_refs': ['e1', 'e8', 
'e10', 'e13', 'e45', 'e56', 'e57', 'e58', 'e14', 'e15', 'e16', 'e54', 'e55'], 
'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x2', 'x3']}, {'chapter_id': 2, 'bundle_token': 
'b2-a2808674', 'content': 'The ring is a gold signet ring with an engraved 
bezel. The supplied sources identify an illustration of it as “Ring of Nestor. 
CMS VI, 277,” while a separate source records Evans’s drawing from a Knossos 
notebook and a watercolour made by Emile Gilliéron fils.[1][2] The available 
material does not securely establish the ring’s measurements: a 
catalogue-related source identifies the ring as no. 277 but assigns 
measurements of 2.1 centimetres by 1.2 centimetres to no. 278, so those figures
cannot be attributed to the Ring of Nestor without qualification.[3] The ring’s
commonly used name derives from its reported association with the Pylos region,
identified in the sources with the legendary home of Nestor.[4]\n\nA 2019 study
describes the ring as allegedly discovered in a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near 
Pylos and records its first appearance in scholarly literature in 1925.[5] The 
same study states that Evans privately purchased the object and published it in
the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*, whereas members of the archaeological board 
in Athens had previously refused to buy it because they considered it a forgery
made by a modern artist.[6] The discovery account therefore belongs to the 
object’s reported history rather than to a securely verified excavation 
provenance. In the study’s discussion of *gemmae dubitandae*, the absence of 
professional, well-documented archaeological excavations is given as a reason 
for withholding confidence in Bronze Age attribution.[7]', 'evidence_refs': 
['e5', 'e6', 'e12', 'e13', 'e14', 'e1', 'e3', 'e4', 'e5', 'e6', 'e7', 'e8', 
'e15'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x3']}]})
