'b3-d5fdbb58', 'content': 'The ring’s bezel carries a miniature engraved scene.
Evans interpreted the pictorial composition as a Minoan Elysian domain in which
a human couple journeys to the Beyond and enters the Elysian Fields.[15] In 
that reading, butterflies and chrysalises could symbolize the human soul; a 
later account of Evans’s interpretation connects the soul with weighing and 
possible reincarnation.[15][16] The available evidence presents these as 
historical interpretations attributed to Evans, not as an independently 
established reconstruction of Minoan religious belief.\n\nThe object is 
catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277.[6][9] A 2020 study identifies a pictorial unit 
previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a scorpion and interprets 
it as an apotropaic figure that kills the ring’s “Minoan Dragon.”[6] Because 
the accessible material supplies the study’s abstract but not its complete 
visual analysis, this identification should be treated as that study’s 
interpretation rather than as an uncontested description. The record also 
