{'chapter_id': 4, 'bundle_token': 'b4-c266dae0', 'content': 'The authenticity 
question was present from the object’s first documented scholarly circulation. 
A source states that the ring was found in 1924 and that Arthur Evans accepted 
it as authentic, whereas many other scholars condemned it as a forgery.[4] A 
more specific account reports that members of the archaeological board in 
Athens refused to buy it because they considered it “a forgery manufactured by 
a modern artist”; Evans nevertheless privately purchased it and published it in
the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*.[15] The board’s assessment records a 
historical judgment by its members, while Evans’s publication records his 
contrary position; neither fact by itself establishes the object’s modern or 
Bronze Age manufacture.\n\nArguments against authenticity include the 
suspicious circumstances of the reported discovery and the lack of a 
professionally documented excavation provenance.[4][15] Marinatos and Jackson 
further argue that the ring’s interpretive coherence depends on Egyptian 
prototypes and that its butterfly motif weighs against authenticity; the 
supplied text provides their abstract-level conclusion but not the detailed 
comparisons.[4] The term *gemma dubitanda* captures this evidentiary position: 
it refers to sealstones and signet rings attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age 
whose provenance and authenticity remain uncertain.[15]\n\nThe dispute has not 
been uniform across later writing. The abstract of a 2020 study by A. G. 
