│                               Ring of Nestor                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The Ring of Nestor is the name commonly given to a gold signet ring that       
entered scholarly literature in 1925 and became associated with interpretations
of Minoan imagery and the afterlife.[1][3] Sir Arthur Evans published a 1925   
study titled The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a   
Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbe,           
Boeotia.[1][3] The ring’s reported discovery in 1924 and its association with a
tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos are not supported in the supplied material 
by a professionally documented excavation record.[4][15] Its authenticity has  
received conflicting assessments: Evans accepted it and used its imagery in a  
Minoan afterlife interpretation, while members of the archaeological board in  
Athens reportedly rejected it as a modern forgery.[15] A later peer-reviewed   
article by Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson argued that the ring’s butterfly 
motif weighs against authenticity, although the available abstract does not    
provide the full iconographic analysis.[4] The reported Kakovatos find context 
is therefore not equivalent to verified provenance, and the opposing historical
assessments do not establish a settled authentication result.[15] These        
questions place the object within the category gemma dubitanda, a technical    
term for sealstones and signet rings attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age whose 
provenance is not securely documented.[15]                                     


                      Description and reported provenance                      

The ring is a gold signet ring with an engraved bezel. The supplied sources    
identify an illustration of it as “Ring of Nestor. CMS VI, 277,” while another 
record documents Evans’s drawing and a watercolour associated with the         
object.[22] The ring’s commonly used name derives from its reported association
with the Pylos region, identified in the sources with the legendary home of    
Nestor.[21]                                                                    

A 2019 study describes the ring as allegedly discovered in a tholos tomb at    
Kakovatos near Pylos and records its first appearance in scholarly literature  
in 1925.[15] The same study states that Evans privately purchased the object   
and published it in the Journal of Hellenic Studies, whereas members of the    
archaeological board in Athens had previously refused to buy it because they   
considered it a forgery made by a modern artist.[15] The discovery account     
therefore belongs to the object’s reported history rather than to a securely   
verified excavation provenance. In the study’s discussion of gemmae dubitandae,
the absence of professional, well-documented archaeological excavations is     
given as a reason for withholding confidence in Bronze Age attribution.[15]    


                        Iconography and interpretation                         

The ring’s bezel carries a miniature engraved scene. Evans interpreted the     
pictorial composition as a Minoan Elysian domain in which a human couple       
journeys to the Beyond and enters the Elysian Fields.[15] In that reading,     
butterflies and chrysalises could symbolize the human soul; a later account of 
Evans’s interpretation connects the soul with weighing and possible            
reincarnation.[15][16] These are historical interpretations attributed to      
Evans, not an independently established reconstruction of Minoan religious     
belief.                                                                        

The object is catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277.[6][9] A 2020 study identifies a   
pictorial unit previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a scorpion 
and interprets it as an apotropaic figure that kills the ring’s “Minoan        
Dragon.”[6] Because the accessible material supplies the study’s abstract but  
not its complete visual analysis, this identification should be treated as that
study’s interpretation rather than as an uncontested description. The available
record documents Evans’s drawing, a colour watercolour, and comparisons with   
other imagery, but the supplied excerpts do not provide the full arguments     
attached to those illustrations.[6]                                            


                              Authenticity debate                              

The authenticity question was present from the object’s first documented       
scholarly circulation. A source states that the ring was found in 1924 and that
Arthur Evans accepted it as authentic, whereas many other scholars condemned it
as a forgery.[4] A more specific account reports that members of the           
archaeological board in Athens refused to buy it because they considered it “a 
forgery manufactured by a modern artist”; Evans nevertheless privately         
purchased it and published it in the Journal of Hellenic Studies.[15] These    
were conflicting historical assessments: the board’s judgment and Evans’s      
publication document opposing positions, but neither fact by itself establishes
the object’s modern or Bronze Age manufacture.[15]                             

Arguments against authenticity include the suspicious circumstances of the     
reported discovery and the lack of a professionally documented excavation      
provenance.[4][15] Marinatos and Jackson further argue that the ring’s         
interpretive coherence depends on Egyptian prototypes and that its butterfly   
motif weighs against authenticity; the supplied text provides their            
abstract-level conclusion but not the detailed comparisons.[4] The term gemma  
dubitanda captures this evidentiary position: it refers to sealstones and      
signet rings attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age whose provenance and          
authenticity remain uncertain.[15]                                             

The dispute has not been uniform across later writing. The supplied abstract of
a 2020 study states that earlier identifications of certain gold rings,        
including the Ring of Nestor, were reassessed in favour of authenticity on the 
basis of comparable iconographic finds and more detailed study of technique and
craftsmanship.[4] The available material therefore records a later             
pro-authenticity reassessment alongside continuing uncertainty about the ring’s
provenance and manufacture; it does not establish a settled authentication     
result.[4][15]                                                                 


